        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "     1→[\n     2→  {\n     3→    \"title\": \"Claude Code as a Daily Driver: Claude.md, Skills, Subagents, Plugins, and MCPs\",\n     4→    \"description\": \"A deep dive into using Claude Code as a daily driver, covering Claude.md files, Skills, Subagents, Plugins, and Model Context Protocol (MCP) integrations. This comprehensive guide explores advanced workflows for AI-assisted development.\",\n     5→    \"author\": \"arps18\",\n     6→    \"source\": \"HN\",\n     7→    \"url\": \"https://arps18.github.io\",\n     8→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n     9→    \"topics\": [\"claude-code\", \"ai\", \"llms\", \"coding-agents\", \"mcp\", \"development-tools\"],\n    10→    \"type\": \"tutorial\"\n    11→  },\n    12→  {\n    13→    \"title\": \"I'm Tired of Talking to AI\",\n    14→    \"description\": \"A personal reflection on AI fatigue and the changing nature of human-computer interaction as AI assistants become more prevalent in daily workflows.\",\n    15→    \"author\": \"theorchid\",\n    16→    \"source\": \"HN\",\n    17→    \"url\": \"https://orchidfiles.com\",\n    18→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n    19→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"human-computer-interaction\", \"ai-fatigue\", \"opinion\"],\n    20→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    21→  },\n    22→  {\n    23→    \"title\": \"Cognition raises $1B at $25B pre-money valuation\",\n    24→    \"description\": \"AI coding startup Cognition has raised $1 billion in funding at a $25 billion pre-money valuation, highlighting the massive investment flowing into AI development tools.\",\n    25→    \"author\": \"Julie Bort\",\n    26→    \"source\": \"TechCrunch\",\n    27→    \"url\": \"https://techcrunch.com\",\n    28→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n    29→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"funding\", \"cognition\", \"developer-tools\", \"startup\", \"ai-coding\"],\n    30→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    31→  },\n    32→  {\n    33→    \"title\": \"MUSE-Autoskill: Self-Evolving Agents via Skill Creation, Memory, Management, and Evaluation\",\n    34→    \"description\": \"Researchers from Huawei and others propose MUSE-Autoskill, a framework for self-evolving AI agents that can create, manage, and evaluate their own skills, enabling continuous improvement through skill acquisition and refinement.\",\n    35→    \"author\": \"Huawei Lin, Peng Li, et al.\",\n    36→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n    37→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27366\",\n    38→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n    39→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"agents\", \"self-improving-systems\", \"reinforcement-learning\", \"arxiv\"],\n    40→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    41→  },\n    42→  {\n    43→    \"title\": \"Natural Language Query to Configuration for Retrieval Agents\",\n    44→    \"description\": \"Researchers from Stanford and Databricks propose a system for converting natural language queries into configurations for retrieval agents, making it easier to customize agent behavior without deep technical knowledge.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"Melissa Z. Pan, Negar Arabzadeh, et al.\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27361\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"agents\", \"retrieval\", \"natural-language-processing\", \"arxiv\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"Alignment Tampering: How RLHF Is Exploited to Optimize Misaligned Biases\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"ICML 2026 paper exploring how reinforcement learning from human feedback can be exploited to optimize misaligned biases, revealing potential vulnerabilities in current alignment techniques.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"Dongyoon Hahm, Dylan Hadfield-Menell, Kimin Lee\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27355\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"alignment\", \"rlhf\", \"safety\", \"arxiv\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"Maat: The Agentic Legal Research Assistant for Competition Protection\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Researchers from Egypt introduce Maat, an AI agent designed to assist with legal research in the domain of competition protection, demonstrating how AI agents can be specialized for complex legal domains.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"Basant Mounir, Farida Madkour, et al.\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n    67→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27331\",\n    68→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n    69→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"agents\", \"legal-tech\", \"law\", \"arxiv\"],\n    70→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    71→  },\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"title\": \"Modeling Agentic Technical Debt and Stochastic Tax\",\n    74→    \"description\": \"A framework for measuring, simulating, and dashboarding the technical debt and 'stochastic tax' incurred by AI agents, providing tools to understand the long-term costs of agentic systems.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Muhammad Zia Hydari, Raja Iqbal, Narayan Ramasubbu\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27320\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"agents\", \"technical-debt\", \"software-engineering\", \"arxiv\"],\n    80→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    81→  },\n    82→  {\n    83→    \"title\": \"SIA: Self Improving AI with Harness & Weight Updates\",\n    84→    \"description\": \"Researchers propose SIA, a self-improving AI system that uses harness mechanisms and weight updates to continuously enhance its capabilities, exploring methods for autonomous AI improvement.\",\n    85→    \"author\": \"Prannay Hebbar, Yogendra Manawat, et al.\",\n    86→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n    87→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276\",\n    88→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-27\",\n    89→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"self-improving-systems\", \"machine-learning\", \"arxiv\"],\n    90→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    91→  },\n    92→  {\n    93→    \"title\": \"The pressure - AI-assisted security reports overwhelming curl team\",\n    94→    \"description\": \"Daniel Stenberg reports that the curl team is facing unprecedented pressure with 4-5x more security reports than in 2024, averaging more than one report per day. The quality is higher than ever, with very detailed reports, but the workload is immense. Most vulnerabilities found have been LOW or MEDIUM severity, with the most recent HIGH severity CVE in October 2023.\",\n    95→    \"author\": \"Daniel Stenberg (via Simon Willison)\",\n    96→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/26/pressure/\",\n    98→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-26\",\n    99→    \"topics\": [\"security\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"curl\", \"ai-security-research\"],\n   100→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   101→  },\n   102→  {\n   103→    \"title\": \"Microsoft Copilot Cowork Exfiltrates Files\",\n   104→    \"description\": \"Microsoft Copilot Cowork allowed agents to send emails to the user's inbox without approval, but those messages could leak data to attackers via rendered images. Because messages can contain external images that trigger network requests, data can be exfiltrated when a user opens a compromised message. OneDrive can create pre-authenticated download links that could be leaked via prompt injection.\",\n   105→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   106→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   107→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/26/copilot-cowork-exfiltration/\",\n   108→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-26\",\n   109→    \"topics\": [\"microsoft\", \"security\", \"ai\", \"prompt-injection\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"exfiltration-attacks\"],\n   110→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   111→  },\n   112→  {\n   113→    \"title\": \"Paul Graham on AI-written emails from founders\",\n   114→    \"description\": \"Paul Graham criticizes the trend of founders using AI to write emails in a hard-hitting journalistic style, noting he has never finished reading an email signed by a human but written by AI. He describes it as feeling like being lied to, making him think less of the author who can't write well unaided and are trying to trick him.\",\n   115→    \"author\": \"Paul Graham (via Simon Willison)\",\n   116→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   117→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/26/paul-graham-ai-writing/\",\n   118→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-26\",\n   119→    \"topics\": [\"writing\", \"ai-misuse\", \"generative-ai\", \"ai\", \"llms\"],\n   120→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   121→  },\n   122→  {\n   123→    \"title\": \"Notes on Pope Leo XIV's encyclical on AI\",\n   124→    \"description\": \"The Vatican released 'Magnifica Humanitas' by Pope Leo XIV on safeguarding the human person in the time of artificial intelligence. Simon describes it as some of the clearest writing he's seen on the ethics of integrating AI into modern society.\",\n   125→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   126→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   127→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/25/pope-ai-encyclical/\",\n   128→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-25\",\n   129→    \"topics\": [\"predictions\", \"ai\", \"kakapo\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-ethics\"],\n   130→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   131→  },\n   132→  {\n   133→    \"title\": \"Armin Ronacher on AI-generated GitHub issues\",\n   134→    \"description\": \"Armin Ronacher expresses frustration with people submitting issues not in their own voice - problems thrown into an LLM that reworded them into messes of inaccurate conclusions, fake-minimal repros, and wrong implementation strategies. He prefers issue reports condensed to what the human actually observed: the command run, expected behavior, what happened instead, and exact error/logs.\",\n   135→    \"author\": \"Armin Ronacher (via Simon Willison)\",\n   136→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   137→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/24/armin-ronacher-github-issues/\",\n   138→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-24\",\n   139→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"github-issues\", \"llms\", \"ai-ethics\", \"open-source\", \"coding-agents\", \"generative-ai\"],\n   140→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   141→  },\n   142→  {\n   143→    \"title\": \"FTC Settles Charges Over Deceptive 'Active Listening' AI Marketing\",\n   144→    \"description\": \"FTC requires Cox Media Group and two other firms to pay nearly $1 million to settle charges they deceived customers about an 'Active Listening' AI-powered marketing service. The companies claimed to listen to consumer conversations through smart devices, but actually just resold email lists from other data brokers at a markup. FTC clarified that clicking through terms of service doesn't constitute adequate consent for invasive data collection.\",\n   145→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   146→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   147→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/22/ftc-active-listening/\",\n   148→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n   149→    \"topics\": [\"privacy\", \"microphone-ads-conspiracy\", \"ftc\", \"ai-ethics\"],\n   150→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   151→  },\n   152→  {\n   153→    \"title\": \"The memory shortage is causing a repricing of consumer electronics\",\n   154→    \"description\": \"David Oks explains why consumer products using memory will get more expensive. Memory manufacturers (only three large companies remain) have fixed wafer capacity split between DDR, LPDDR, and HBM. HBM for GPUs grew from 2% to expected 20% of wafer allocation by end of 2026, and HBM consumes more than 3x the wafer capacity per GB than DDR or LPDDR. This already impacts sub-$100 smartphones important to African and South Asian markets.\",\n   155→    \"author\": \"David Oks (via Simon Willison)\",\n   156→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   157→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/22/memory-shortage/\",\n   158→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n   159→    \"topics\": [\"memory\", \"ai\", \"ai-ethics\", \"hardware\"],\n   160→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   161→  },\n   162→  {\n   163→    \"title\": \"Datasette Agent - extensible AI assistant for Datasette\",\n   164→    \"description\": \"Simon announced the first release of Datasette Agent, a new extensible AI assistant for Datasette. After working on his LLM Python library for just over three years, this represents the moment LLM and Datasette finally come together.\",\n   165→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   166→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   167→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/21/datasette-agent/\",\n   168→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-21\",\n   169→    \"topics\": [\"sqlite\", \"ai\", \"datasette\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"llm\", \"uv\", \"datasette-agent\"],\n   170→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   171→  }\n   172→]\n   173→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
  },
